[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: [1mwhat "care about" actually means.[0m This is the heart of the domain model — it decides what the subscription table
  looks like and whether users get silence or a firehose.

  - [1mImplicit[0m — you're automatically subscribed to tasks you created, are assigned to, or have commented on. Zero UI to
    build, and it matches what people expect without being taught. The risk is surprise: users get notified about a
    thread they commented on once and don't understand why.
  - [1mExplicit[0m — a "watch" button per task. Perfectly predictable and trivially simple to model. The failure mode is worse
    than it sounds: nobody remembers to click watch, so the system is silent and people conclude it's broken.
  - [1mHybrid[0m — implicit auto-subscribe, plus an explicit watch/unwatch toggle that overrides it. This is what GitHub and
    Linear both converged on, which is weak evidence but consistent evidence.

  The expensive-to-change part is the subscription record itself. Implicit-only can store nothing and derive subscribers
  at send time from creator/assignee/commenter; the moment you want mute, you need a real table with an explicit state
  per user per task, and derived-at-send-time logic has to be rewritten. Going hybrid from the start costs one small
  table and saves that rewrite.

  My recommendation: [1mhybrid[0m, modeled as an explicit subscription row with states [38;5;153mauto[39m, [38;5;153mwatching[39m, [38;5;153mmuted[39m. Implicit rules
  create [38;5;153mauto[39m rows; the toggle writes [38;5;153mwatching[39m or [38;5;153mmuted[39m. One mechanism, no special cases at send time.
